And so the burden cannot just be on the individual to pull out, but there really has to be a collective solution that I believe organizations need to be responsible for. One of the ideas that you have proposed Gloria is that we all have fluctuations in our capacity for attention during the day. Talk about the idea of these attention rhythms and how we can take advantage of them. When most people think of attention, they think of there being two states, you're focused or unfocused. And as I've been studying attention, it occurred to me that there's another variable in the mix that's really important.

65
00:42:49.000 --> 00:43:24.000
And that is how much mental effort is involved when you're paying attention. So there's things we do that can be very challenging, like trying to read a difficult article. And there's things we do that are very easy, like playing solitaire or playing a pangram game. So we did a research study where we asked people questions throughout the day. And people had to simply respond how engaged were you and the thing you were just doing

66
00:43:24.000 --> 00:44:05.000
and how challenged were you and the thing you were just doing and how challenged were you and the thing you were just doing and so people responded we had time stamps and we contract their responses over the course of the day. Turns out that when we look at the focused state of attention being challenged and engaged we see a peak rhythm which is around mid-morning for most people and again about mid afternoon for most people. There are individual differences of course, but we do see that there are these peaks and valleys, so to speak, of attention throughout the day. How do we take advantage of that?

67
00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:41.000
So if we have a number of tasks during the day and we know that we have these rhythms of attention, how should we marry the levels of our attention with the tasks that we have to accomplish? Think about what your peak focus times are. And think about planning to do those tasks that require the hardest work, the most thought, the most creative thinking during those times when your attention is at its peak. And for the times when our attention isn't a valley, that's when I do what I call subordinate work.

